<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Gajaaga</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1858 CE (approx.) · capital Maxanna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Soninke kingdom of the upper Senegal river, called Galam by the French, a hub of the gum, gold and slave trades.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gajaaga was founded by the Bacili clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soninke">Soninke</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> around the junction with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faleme-river">Faleme</a> river, an area rich in gold from nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambuk">Bambuk</a>. It emerged as an independent kingdom in the 17th century as larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> empires declined.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The ruler, the Tunka, was chosen from the Bacili lineages and ruled from Maxanna and other river towns. The French built Fort Saint-Joseph nearby around 1700 and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakel">Bakel</a>, and Gajaaga traded slaves, gum arabic, gold and grain down the river to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soninke">Soninke</a> merchants and clerics were active traders across the region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Civil wars in the 18th and 19th centuries left Gajaaga divided into two parts, Goye and Kammera. The French extended their control and placed the area under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1858, and it became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1600 CE – 1858 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.54%;width:5.13%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1858 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Maxanna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Soninke</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a>, <a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gajaaga, Galam, Gadiaga</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1729&amp;focus=kingdom-of-gajaaga" aria-label="Kingdom of Gajaaga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4277 1607 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Gajaaga lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4597 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4597 1863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Senegal. <a href="/atlas?year=1729&amp;focus=kingdom-of-gajaaga">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

